> Murray Beach[ Provincial] Park is a campground located in Little Shemogue. Murray Beach Provincial Park located in Murray[ Corner], just off[ route] 955, is one of New[ Brunswick]'s best kept secrets. Large, spacious sites, two-way hook ups, a playground and laundry facility make this a wonderful family vacation location.
Murray Beach is an ideal spot to relax during your tour of the southeastern portion of New Brunswick's Acadian[ Coastal] Drive. In particular, you'll want to take in the[ breathtaking] view of sunrises over dramatic ocean waters. This provincial park is just a few kilometres east of the spectacular Cape Jourimain Nature Centre and the Confederation Bridge. Birdwatchers will fall in love with the avian bounty to be found in this area. There are lots of activities and attractions in the vicinity, including sea kayaking, farmers markets, lighthouses, covered bridges, festivals and historic sites. Murray Beach Provincial Park has an inviting community seaside campground with playground, picnic areas and an unsupervised saltwater beach that offers change houses and washrooms. Murray Beach is truly one of New[ Brunswick]'s best-kept secrets. A wonderful family vacation location. Find out for yourself!
The information displayed here is displayed 'as is'. Also check the website ( if listed ) for up-to-date information, prices and availability. Stay Canada is not responsible for the content of external websites. If you are the owner or operator of Murray Beach Provincial Park and wish to update or modify the content on this page including room details, specials and getaways or availability, please use our submission page.
Positives[:] Location, nature, loads of space.
Negatives[:] Season is too short. Should be open into the fall.
Comments[:] This campground is a gem. The sites are spacious and the park setting is beautiful. There are open sites as well as wooded ones. The walking trails are nice and nothing beats a walk along the shore doing some beach combing. The staff are friendly and very helpful. The sandbars are great fun at low tide. But the most amazing feature of the park is the incredible sunsets. Breat[htaking]!
